Use case: concept-art. Asset type: visual identity reference for a physical cardboard puppet musical.
Create a beautiful melancholic French travelling marionette theatre in a storm at dusk. Two LIVING adult marionettes perform on worn wooden boards: a proud female dancer in a faded deep petrol-blue ballet dress with a SHORT scalloped skirt above the knees, cream stockings and worn ballet shoes; and a shy male backstage clown in an ivory shirt, patched slate-blue waistcoat, loose charcoal trousers and soft work shoes. Her hair is chestnut in a small bun, her face strong and tender, his face long and gentle with a tiny faded red clown nose. Both have ordinary short necks tucked into collars, well-proportioned heads, expressive hands and very visible brass-pin elbows and knees. Her long crimson costume sash passes between their hands as they counterbalance across a broken step. He has an old broom nearby, a small battered hat and a wooden stool. Their faces show fear and affection under grief, never a delighted grin. No children. Both alive, no ghost.
Style: exquisite hand-painted gouache, muted ink contour, vintage French lithography and cut cardboard toy theatre. Physical paper grain, scuffed paint and tarnished gold, restrained painterly highlights. Flat front-facing theatrical perspective with readable silhouettes, NOT 3D rendered dolls, NOT photoreal people. Thin visible black marionette strings go up into the dark canvas roof. A torn roof canopy, crooked painted cloud wings, bare backstage crossbar and packing trunk indicate the theatre is closing permanently. Wide view, entire bodies and shoes visible, limbs clearly readable. Dark storm blue, dusty ivory, faded teal, oxblood red and old ochre. A little warm footlight on their faces, great sorrow and dignity. No magical rescue, no celebratory confetti. No text, lettering, logo, watermark or typography. This image establishes the cast and paint style; subsequent separate asset sheets must preserve them.
